WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.234
A Bash script should invoke the AWS CLI in
a predictable and reviewable way.

2
00:00:07.314 --> 00:00:14.690
The safer pattern is a fixed command
structure with clearly separated options and

3
00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:15.732
operands.

4
00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:23.508
Avoid building one large command string
from variable data and then re-interpreting

5
00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:25.512
it through the shell.

6
00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:31.792
That approach makes quoting harder, hides
behavior from reviewers,

7
00:00:31.872 --> 00:00:37.644
and can produce surprising results when an
input is unexpected.

8
00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:41.465
Quote values that should remain literal,

9
00:00:41.545 --> 00:00:47.959
and use arrays where the script needs to
assemble options and operands.

10
00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:54.666
Validate service names, operation names,
ARNs, regions, profile names,

11
00:00:54.746 --> 00:01:01.908
resource identifiers, file paths, output
paths, filters, query expressions,

12
00:01:01.988 --> 00:01:04.767
and timeout values before use.

13
00:01:04.847 --> 00:01:11.047
Validation should favor known allowed
values and expected formats,

14
00:01:11.127 --> 00:01:16.792
especially for scripts that can affect
production resources.

15
00:01:16.872 --> 00:01:22.751
Working directory control matters when
scripts read templates,

16
00:01:22.831 --> 00:01:28.817
policy documents, parameter files,
artifacts, or JSON payloads.

17
00:01:28.897 --> 00:01:34.349
A relative path should resolve from the
intended directory,

18
00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:38.704
not from wherever the script happened to
start.

19
00:01:38.784 --> 00:01:44.663
Tool version expectations also matter
because AWS CLI behavior,

20
00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:48.271
output formats, paginated responses,

21
00:01:48.351 --> 00:01:54.444
and service features can vary across
versions and configurations.

22
00:01:54.524 --> 00:01:58.692
Result handling is part of secure
automation.

23
00:01:58.772 --> 00:02:01.872
Scripts should check exit status,

24
00:02:01.952 --> 00:02:06.656
distinguish expected empty results from
failures,

25
00:02:06.736 --> 00:02:11.546
and stop before later steps trust
incomplete output.

26
00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:15.795
Clear error messages help operators
recover,

27
00:02:15.875 --> 00:02:21.647
but those messages should not expose
secrets, raw credentials,

28
00:02:21.727 --> 00:02:27.178
sensitive resource details, or excessive
response bodies.

29
00:02:27.258 --> 00:02:34.420
A secure AWS CLI wrapper is
understandable, strict, and intentionally boring.
